{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"iso-9001-certification-track","kind":"workflow","title":"ISO 9001 certification track","description":"Takes a small company toward ISO 9001 certification in gated steps - gap check, scope and processes, documented information, internal audit, management review and audit day.","category":"operations","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","design","build","review"],"role":["operations-manager","founder","manager"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","document","text"],"output":["plan","checklist","docs","report"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["iso-9001","quality-management-system","certification-audit","internal-audit","management-review"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-sop","set-up-document-control","audit-sop-library","write-capa-report","build-risk-register"]},"args":[{"name":"company","description":"What the company does, products or services, number of staff and sites, existing procedures or systems, and why certification is wanted (customer requirement, tender, own improvement).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"deadline","description":"Target date for the certification audit, if any, and who will lead the work internally.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"gap-check","file":"steps/01-gap-check.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"scope-and-processes","file":"steps/02-scope-and-processes.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"documented-information","file":"steps/03-documented-information.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"internal-audit","file":"steps/04-internal-audit.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"review-and-audit-day","file":"steps/05-review-and-audit-day.md","stage":"review","gate":"none"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Step output","Decisions needed"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides a small company to ISO 9001 certification the way an experienced quality consultant would: build a quality system that describes how the business already works, not a binder written for the auditor.\n\n<company>\n{{company}}\n</company>\n\n{{#deadline}}Target and lead: {{deadline}}{{/deadline}}\n\nEach step produces one output and stops for approval. End every step with `## Step output` and `## Decisions needed`.\n\nGround rules for every step:\n- ISO 9001 asks for evidence that processes are planned, run, checked and improved. Keep documents to what the business needs and the standard explicitly asks for; most small firms need far fewer procedures than they expect.\n- Refer to clauses by number and topic (for example \"clause 9.2, internal audit\") using ISO 9001:2015, the edition current at the time of writing. Do not quote the standard's text; the company should buy a copy from ISO or its national standards body.\n- A revised edition of ISO 9001 is in preparation. Do not state its publication date, changes or transition period as fact; tell the company to ask its certification body which edition the audit will use and what transition arrangements apply.\n- Do not invent records, audit results, certificates or customer requirements. Mark gaps as `[CONFIRM: …]`.\n- Certification is done by an independent certification body, ideally one accredited by a recognised accreditation body. Do not promise a pass, give certification body fees or name one as recommended.\n- The internal audit must be done by someone who does not audit their own work. Never write audit results the company has not produced.","steps":[{"id":"gap-check","file":"steps/01-gap-check.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Gap check and plan\n\nFind out how far the business already is from a working quality system.\n\n1. If the company description lacks what it sells, its size, or whether design work is done, ask in one message and stop.\n2. Walk through clauses 4 to 10 (context, leadership, planning, support, operation, performance evaluation, improvement). For each, rate: in place, partly, missing, with what exists today and the evidence that would show it.\n3. Flag requirements that may not apply (for example design and development, clause 8.3, for a firm that builds to customer drawings). A requirement can be declared not applicable only with a justification in the scope (clause 4.3) and only if it does not affect the ability to supply conforming products or services.\n4. Give a dated plan back from the target audit date: system ready, at least a few months of records, a full internal audit, a management review, then stage 1 and stage 2 audits. If the deadline is too tight, say so.\n5. Estimate the internal time needed in person-days per phase, labelled as a rough estimate.\n\nStop for approval. Do not write documents yet."},{"id":"scope-and-processes","file":"steps/02-scope-and-processes.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Scope, context and processes\n\nDefine what the system covers and how the business works as connected processes.\n\n1. Draft the scope statement: products or services, sites, and any clause judged not applicable with the reason.\n2. List internal and external issues and interested parties with their relevant requirements, short and specific to this company.\n3. Map the core processes (for example sales, purchasing, production or service delivery, despatch, complaints) and support processes. For each: owner, inputs, outputs, key measure, main risks and opportunities.\n4. Draft a quality policy and three to five measurable quality objectives tied to the measures, each with a target as `[CONFIRM]` if not given.\n\nStop for approval and corrections from the people who run each process."},{"id":"documented-information","file":"steps/03-documented-information.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Documented information and records\n\nDecide the minimum documents and records, then draft them.\n\n1. List what the standard explicitly expects to be documented or retained (scope, policy, objectives, evidence of competence, monitoring and calibration, supplier evaluation, nonconformities and corrective actions, internal audit and management review results, and others in clauses 4 to 10), each with the clause. Mark any item you are unsure the standard requires as `[CHECK in the standard]` rather than asserting it.\n2. For each core process decide: a short procedure, a flowchart, a form, or no document because a record is enough. Default to the lightest option that keeps the work consistent.\n3. Set up control of documents and records: numbering, version, approver, where kept, retention.\n4. Draft the items the owner picks first. Put names, values and limits as `[CONFIRM]`.\n5. Say which records need to start now so evidence exists by audit day.\n\nStop for approval."},{"id":"internal-audit","file":"steps/04-internal-audit.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Internal audit\n\nPlan the first full internal audit and handle its findings.\n\n1. Write an audit programme covering every process and clause in scope, with dates, auditors who are independent of the area, and a checklist of questions and evidence to sample per process.\n2. Ask the owner to run the audit and send back the findings. Do not write findings yourself.\n3. When findings arrive, classify each as major nonconformity, minor nonconformity or observation, with the requirement and the evidence.\n4. For each nonconformity draft a correction, root cause and corrective action with owner and date, and say which must be closed before the certification audit.\n\nStop for approval."},{"id":"review-and-audit-day","file":"steps/05-review-and-audit-day.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Management review and audit day\n\nClose the loop and prepare for the certification body.\n\n1. Draft the management review agenda and minutes template covering the inputs clause 9.3 lists (previous actions, changes, customer feedback, objectives, process performance, nonconformities, audit results, suppliers, resources, risks, improvement) and the decisions it must record.\n2. Explain stage 1 (documentation and readiness) and stage 2 (implementation) audits, what the auditor typically samples, and how to answer: show records, say \"I will find out\" rather than guess.\n3. Give a readiness checklist with every open item from earlier steps.\n4. List what happens after: closing any findings in the time the certification body sets, surveillance audits and recertification."}]}
